00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
hi there steve kaufman  talking about languages again  there are many myths in language  learning one of the most damaging is the  idea  that past a certain age we can no longer  learn languages or adults can't learn  languages or as you get older your  ability to learn languages deteriorates  diminishes  not true  it might be  theoretically or somebody might  demonstrate scientifically that  neuroplasticity declines  but you can still learn okay you might  have learned faster at the age of 20 and  a little more slowly at the age of 50  although that has not been my experience  my experience has been that i learned  better now than i when i was 20 because  i had i know how to learn better i'm  more confident now and of course there's  so many more  uh you know resources available to help  you learn so  the reason i raised this subject is  because apparently there was a study  done in a magazine called cognition  which sought to demonstrate they did  some tests which i don't fully  understand but  the result of which the conclusion was  that we can't learn languages to fluency  after the age of 10. all right so let's  start right there and and by the way  this was picked up by time magazine and  by the guardian in england all right  i was at the long fest festival de long  in montreal where you had 400 poly lots  i addressed them and i asked these 400  polyglots in this big you know  auditorium i said how many of you grew  up in a bi in a multilingual family in  other words we're in a situation where  you were hearing lots of languages i  think one or two people put up their  hands so overwhelmingly these several  hundred people learned these languages  as adults and they many of them spoke  five six ten plus languages so it's not  just me and it's the same when i visit  the polyglot gatherings in europe most  people learn most of their languages as  adults  now  in the time magazine they quote the  study and talk about how you know that  they  quizzed people on their reaction to  certain english phrasing and that this  would demonstrate that they were either  you know german speakers or french  speakers or whatever uh fluency as i  said before if you are a german speaker  speaking english there's a high  probability that there are certain  phrases that will reflect your german  like i said you know i've been living in  canada since many years i can hear  people say that to me in flawless  english people who have wonderful  pronunciation who can read any novel in  the book who are totally conversed in  the language and there are certain  structures that somehow are they're  stuck with whatever came to them from  their native language so what they're  still fluent speakers of the language  all right uh in the guardian article  about this cognition  study  the fellow says you know i'm not going  to accept that  i can't learn i'm going to go try and  learn and then he spends the rest of the  article talking about duolingo  duolingo is not going to get you to  fluency duolingo is sort of an initiator  if you are  not strongly motivated to learn you can  get onto duolingo do some very basic  simple things and have the impression  that you are getting  a bit of a not even a toehold but a bit  of an entry into the language ultimately  if you want to learn you're going to  have to expose yourself to lots and lots  of content and this is the other thing  that's missing whenever they talk about  what you can learn as an adult or  whatever they always look at people who  study at schools you don't learn a  language in a classroom  as manfred spitzer  famous german neuroscientist whom i  often quote said we don't learn  languages in a classroom we learn  languages in our brain so the question  becomes how do we get the language into  the brain and this comes down to how do  we motivate people  how do we motivate people to do enough  with the language in the language so  that the brain starts to get used to the  language that needn't be  limited to what happens in the classroom  in fact what happens in the classroom  what's most important there is to what  extent can the teacher or the classroom  environment stimulate encourage guide  support  and make the learner want to learn the  language independently make the learner  want to spend enough time listening and  reading in order to make some progress  and that reminds me of another  thing that i saw on twitter where  stephen krashen said apparently that  there's a lot of talk on the internet or  amongst teachers and saying you know you  should have ninety percent target  language in the classroom  and stephen creston says it doesn't  matter if the 90  target language is incomprehensible to  the student it's not doing them any good  what's more important is  can you get the learner to commit  themselves to doing enough listening and  reading in the language and in that  regard the teacher using the learner's  language sometimes to explain sometimes  to translate sometimes to encourage all  of this can be very valuable because  the most most of the learning will take  place outside the classroom if the  learner is willing to engage with the  language listen read and do things in  the language so and if a learner is  prepared to do those things it doesn't  matter if the learner is eight years old  12 years old 20 years old  60 years old 72 years old it doesn't  matter we can learn  at whatever age  and by the way whatever our nationality  i don't believe that certain national  groups swedes or singaporeans or  ethiopians where there's a high degree  of multilingualism that those people are  naturally more gifted or that they have  some genetic predisposition to learning  languages anybody can learn if they have  the right attitude and if they spend  enough time at it and so  the classroom as sort of the model this  doesn't work because it didn't work in  the classroom is wrong yes ideally  immersion go to the country spend the  day talking to people whatever but  that's not practical for most people so  you create a world of immersion you  create a world of interesting  comprehensible content initially not so  interesting and then you build up to a  point where you can actually be doing a  lot of listening and reading on things  of interest and gradually the brain gets  used to the language so  is there an ideal uh age at which to  learn i don't think so and i wanted to  add one more thing because in this  discussion back and forth and you get  people saying well we just learned the  way kids learn and and the kids don't  read they learn anyway and stuff true  enough  but i can in six months  in most languages i can reach a level of  vocabulary in six months  that takes the child five years or  longer to achieve so the child may be  more fluent the child may pronounce  better but if we're talking about the  ability to communicate and understand  the language we can learn at any age and  we can learn to fluency fluency not  perfection thank you for listening bye  for now  [Music]  you

